% Suit-preference signal on a discard, using a 5-card run (not a
% 6+ card slam-run). S declares 3NT. Dummy has AQ74 of spades —
% declarer will run 4 spade tricks (after the K drops or finesse
% holds). On the 4th spade, one defender is short and discards.
%
% Expected sequence:
%   T1: W leads SJ (top of JT983).
%   T2: dummy SA, S follows.
%   T3: dummy SQ, finesse picking up the J or K. Whoever wins
%        spades, declarer runs 3-4 of them.
%   T4-5: W has JT983 of spades but only 5 cards — discards on
%        round 4 if forced. W's first discard is the test.
%
% A high discard from suit X signals "want suit X", a low one says
% "don't want." With JT983 in spades, W's discards come from H J9
% / D J63 / C QT9. Picking a HIGH diamond suggests "want diamonds"
% (high = high-ranking suit). Picking a LOW heart suggests "want
% nothing" — give count signal instead. Q-Plus's choice tells us
% which signal system applies on short-suit discards.

% ======================================================================
